@* A list with a host handler above it, which is the only arrangement that can
   see where a key stops.

   Every other test in this folder dispatches on the control it is about and
   asserts what that control did. What the two Escape-consuming fields owe their
   hosts is the opposite kind of fact — that the key went no further — and a key
   that went nowhere looks exactly like a key that was heard, unless something
   above the list is counting. So this harness is a host that counts: the div
   around the list records every key that reached it, and a test asserts on that
   record rather than on the absence of an effect.

   It is the shape of a real host and not an invention for a test, and it covers
   both sides of the arrangement. The live one: in the Tasks pane the div around
   an opened entry's detail (TasksPane.razor:1813) carries a keydown handler, and
   the step list sits inside it (:2051) — that is the host whose Escape closed the
   pane on top of an abandoned step title until this boundary went in. The other
   side is a handler above a list of the pane's own rows, which the pane does not
   have: its keydown handlers are on the filter chip group (:182), the selection
   bar (:236) and the four bulk controls (:938, :963, :1009, :1077), and none of
   them is above the entry list (:290). It is one wrapper element away, though,
   and it is what a pane-level way out of the selection mode would have been — the
   thing that could not be offered while an abandoned rename discarded the picked
   set with it, and that the list's OnDismiss hook offers instead. *@

<div class="escape-host" data-testid="host" @onkeydown="OnHostKeyDown">
    <TaskListView Tasks="@Tasks"
                  QuickEdit="true"
                  DirectRename="@DirectRename"
                  OnRename="Rename"
                  OnAdd="Add"
                  OnDismiss="() => Dismissals++"
                  AriaLabel="Tasks"
                  TestId="list" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The always-open field the step lists use, rather than the pencil's.
    /// Both are the same field and the same handler — see <c>TaskItem</c> — and the
    /// boundary is around both, so both are worth dispatching into.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool DirectRename { get; set; }

    public IReadOnlyList<TaskRow> Tasks { get; private set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Every key that reached the host, in order. Keys rather than a
    /// count, so a test can say which one arrived — the point of the boundary is
    /// that it is one key's business and not the whole keyboard's.</summary>
    public List<string> HostKeys { get; } = [];

    public List<TaskRename> Renames { get; } = [];

    public List<string> Added { get; } = [];

    /// <summary>How many times the list said the reader had asked to be let out of
    /// it. A count rather than a flag, because a key heard twice is exactly the
    /// defect the boundary is here to prevent.</summary>
    public int Dismissals { get; private set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        Tasks = [new("a", "First"), new("b", "Second")];

    private void OnHostKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e) => HostKeys.Add(e.Key);

    private void Rename(TaskRename rename)
    {
        Renames.Add(rename);
        Tasks = [.. Tasks.Select(task =>
            string.Equals(task.Id, rename.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal)
                ? task with { Title = rename.Title }
                : task)];
    }

    private void Add(string title)
    {
        Added.Add(title);
        Tasks = [.. Tasks, new TaskRow($"new-{Added.Count}", title)];
    }
}
